<!--Post: 88--><!--Time: 1085539547-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST88"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Clarice<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Clarice<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->5-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, May 25, 2004 - 7:45 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Tom, since you&#39;ve had the Zodiak site for a while, and since some folks theorize that BTK and Zodiak are the same &#34;dude&#34;; I am wondering if you think they are the same person.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 88-->
<!--Post: 106--><!--Time: 1085685801-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST106"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><a href="mailto:webmaster@catchbtk.com" target="_blank"><!--/email--><!--name-->Tom_voigt<!--/name--></b></a>
</a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Tom_voigt<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->5-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, May 27, 2004 - 12:23 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Clarice, I believe BTK was highly influenced by the Zodiac, but that they are separate individuals. I see no evidence to indicate BTK and the Zodiac are one and the same.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 106-->
<!--Post: 116--><!--Time: 1085778395-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST116"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Mike_r<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Mike_r<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->5-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, May 28, 2004 - 2:06 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Hi- <BR> <BR>I think that it is pretty clear that Zodiac and BTK are not one and the same person.  After all, by 1969 Zodiac had perfected the art of getting all of the attention he wanted.  In the late 1970s, BTK was so pathetic that he had to whine about not being able to get the attention that he so desired. <BR> <BR>Mike<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 116-->
<!--Post: 126--><!--Time: 1085792060-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST126"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Ed_neil<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Ed_neil<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->5-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, May 28, 2004 - 5:54 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->In addition to the newspaper accounts and magazine articles, there were at least two books BTK could have had access to in order to copycat the Zodiac killer to some extent: &#34;The California Crime Book&#34; by Robert Colby &#40;September 1971&#41; and &#34;Great Crimes of San Francisco,&#34; edited by Dean W. Dickensheet &#40;1974&#41;. <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 126-->
<!--Post: 155--><!--Time: 1086100385-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST155"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Warren_b<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Warren_b<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->5-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, June 01, 2004 - 7:33 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Would be interesting to know if WSU or city library carried those books back then.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 155-->
<!--Post: 157--><!--Time: 1086112549-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST157"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Ed_neil<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Ed_neil<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->5-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, June 01, 2004 - 10:55 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->If not, they might have been available at any decent bookstore.  These days, you pretty much have to get lucky to get ahold of those; I managed to find two copies &#40;in the same bookstore!&#41; of &#34;Great Crimes of San Francisco,&#34; and was able to order &#34;The California Crime Book&#34; through Amazon.com&#39;s z shops &#40;somewhat fittingly&#41;.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 157-->
<!--Post: 158--><!--Time: 1086115646-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST158"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><a href="mailto:webmaster@catchbtk.com" target="_blank"><!--/email--><!--name-->Tom_voigt<!--/name--></b></a>
</a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Tom_voigt<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->5-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, June 01, 2004 - 11:47 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->BTK wouldn&#39;t have even needed a book; it was all covered in his local newspaper. Take a look at the Zodiac historical newspaper archive I put together for a sample of how widespread the coverage was.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 158-->
<!--Post: 166--><!--Time: 1086273762-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST166"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Sandy<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Sandy<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->5-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, June 03, 2004 - 7:42 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I believe the attention the Z craved, could never be enough for his appetite. So &#34;if&#34; he was in Kansas as the &#34;BTK&#34;, haveing the attention in Calif. as Z, still wouldn&#39;t be enough for him. Why wouldn&#39;t he wonder why he was able to get so much attention in Calif., and not as much in Kansas? After all he was more horrifying in Kansas, than in Calif.or so he thought anyway.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 166-->
<!--Post: 169--><!--Time: 1086297999-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST169"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Bookworm<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Bookworm<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->5-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, June 03, 2004 - 2:26 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->BTK is scarier to me since it seems he&#39;s still around.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 169-->
<!--Post: 174--><!--Time: 1086312626-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST174"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Muskogee<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Muskogee<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->6-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, June 03, 2004 - 6:30 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I think Z was too prideful to suddenly go by another moniker.  And imagine the shock value he&#39;d have if ZODIAC suddenly showed up in Kansas.  I think he&#39;d go for that in a heartbeat over creating a new identity.  <BR> <BR>I do think BTK was an avid &#34;fan&#34; of Zodiac, though.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 174-->
<!--Post: 180--><!--Time: 1086335036-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST180"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->John_prisk<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->John_prisk<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->6-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, June 04, 2004 - 12:43 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I agree with the &#34;avid fan&#34; theory - - The phone call&#40;s&#41; are very much like Zodiac&#39;s calls to Vallejo and Napa PD&#39;s. And I agree with what Sandy said re: attention - - BTK wanted attention but Zodiac NEEDED it.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 180-->
<!--Post: 307--><!--Time: 1087867163-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST307"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Sandy<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Sandy<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->5-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Monday, June 21, 2004 - 6:19 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Lets say I was the Zodiac,and things were starting to quiet down in the mid 70&#39;s I was no longer on the front page. I still want the attention,but the police have been to my home asking me questions about the z crimes.They don&#39;t have enough to arrest me,but if I continue with the same game,I could be caught! I could go to some other town like Santa Rosa and stir it up there, but I won&#39;t be able to send letters or they would know its me again. I will kill a few there and see if those crimes make the front page. That turned out to be not as much fun as I had killing, and sending notes to all the media as well as the police. The only thing I can do is go to some other state,change my m.o. so they won&#39;t catch on to who it is,and I can continue haveing a fun time. All I have to do is give myself a new name,darn I would love to use &#34;Zodiac&#34; again but,then the Calif.police would soon find out I am not liveing at my old address,they would put two and two together.I will just be forced to change my name, along with my way of collecting slaves, and go far away....&#40;I Sandy, see this as a possibity&#41;<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 307-->
<!--Post: 309--><!--Time: 1087873637-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST309"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Rachel<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Rachel<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->5-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Monday, June 21, 2004 - 8:07 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Hi Sandy~ <BR>Have you found anything out that lends credence to Kane being in the Wichita area during the mid to late 70s? For some reason, I lean toward Kane as having more to do with the Z crimes than he&#39;s given credit for. Call it a gut feeling, I guess. <BR>Also, I saw your posts on C&amp;J. Anything new with the &#34;scarred&#34; perp? Thanks &amp; have a great night! <BR>Rachel<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 309-->
<!--Post: 322--><!--Time: 1087969943-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST322"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Sandy<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Sandy<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->5-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, June 22, 2004 - 10:52 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Thanks Rachel, I know he has the money to travel,and I do know some of the places he has gone.Some time in 74 he was told to get out of LasVegas by his boss Allen Dorphman,and I was told or read, that Dorphman was killed in Kansas not long after.Sure anyone could have killed him,but Kane would have the motive and opportunity! I would love to know where in Kansas Dorphman was killed,and the date.As far as the scarred perp,she told me it was on his cheek. My suspect has one on his cheek, and along his jaw line.Short, stocky ,dark brown hair, space between his front teeth, mustache, odd voice,sure are a lot of similaritys !<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 322-->
<!--Post: 348--><!--Time: 1088287855-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST348"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Matt<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Matt<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->6-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, June 26, 2004 - 3:10 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Interesting observation; I was a freshman in high school in Fairfield, Solano County California when Zodiac started his attacks.  The next few years kept the entire community on edge, since three of his attacks were in Solano County &#40;Benecia, Vallejo, Lake Berryessa&#41;Although I left California in 1972, I kept up with the Zodiac updates. I moved to Wichita in 1985 to continue my  Law Enforcement career.  I was amazed to learn the similarities between the two killers. It seems that Tom Voight probably did solve the Zodiac killings and attacks. Perhaps he can &#34;dig in&#34; here.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 348-->
<!--Post: 1382--><!--Time: 1099713588-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST1382"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Tac<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Tac<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->11-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, November 05, 2004 - 7:59 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Matt, <BR> <BR>If you see this post I find your post a facinating story. To have been in the Vallejo area during the Zodiac activities and then to have moved to Wichita only to run into another case that has Zodiac similarities.  .  .   <BR> <BR>In your opinion does the area of the BTK killings remind you of the Northern Calif areas where the Zodiac killed in their topography, rural qualities, or any other similarities ???<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 1382-->
<!--Post: 1383--><!--Time: 1099714129-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST1383"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Tac<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Tac<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->11-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, November 05, 2004 - 8:08 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Sandy, <BR> <BR>If I am not mistaken, Allen Dorfman was a lawyer for the teamsters and he was the mob&#39;s man in Vegas re: the skim oh so many years ago. <BR> <BR>Again if I have the right man, Dorfman was &#34;hit&#34; or executed by the mob. He knew too much and his usefullness was beginning to wane as the days of skimming the casinos was coming under direct attack from the government.  .  .   <BR> <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 1383-->
<!--Post: 1395--><!--Time: 1099942980-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST1395"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Flytodeb<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Flytodeb<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->6-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Monday, November 08, 2004 - 11:43 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Matt is on a sweet vacation and sends his regards! <BR>-Deb <BR> <BR>&#34;Tac, Thanks for the note.  Wichita, Fairfield and Vallejo share some urban similarities except for the small hills in the Bay Area.  In regards to rural similarities, there are no hills outside Wichita.  However, the site of the Lake Herman Rd. killings  is very similar to rural  areas in Kansas.  The major difference in all this is that BTK&#39;s crimes have all occured in urban sites.&#34; <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 1395-->
<!--Post: 1811--><!--Time: 1102902571-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST1811"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Megan<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Megan<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->11-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Sunday, December 12, 2004 - 5:49 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Personally I think that BTK is an admirer of the Zodiac Killer.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 1811-->
<!--Post: 1814--><!--Time: 1102920095-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST1814"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Ivy_leaguer<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Ivy_leaguer<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->10-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Sunday, December 12, 2004 - 10:41 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->The BTK Strangler and the Zodiac Killer are both psychopathic serial murderers who derive pleasure from murdering people, in particular women. Serial murderers are familiar with the modus operandi and any signature traits of other serial murderers. Its possible the BTK Strangler may respect the Zodiac Killer. Richard Ramirez, the Night Stalker appreciated the efforts of Kenneth Bianchi and Angelo Buono. Bianchi and Buono were the Hillside Stranglers. <BR> <BR>The BTK Strangler and the Zodiac Killer are very intelligent individuals who enjoyed manipulating the police and public by corresponding with the police and media organisations. Both want the police and the public to know they possess superior intelligence and flaunt it in poetry, puzzles and daring the police to apprehend them. <BR> <!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 1814-->
<!--Post: 1876--><!--Time: 1103069264-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST1876"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Tac<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Tac<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->11-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, December 14, 2004 - 4:07 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Ivy, <BR> <BR>BTK is known as BTK not BTK Strangler. BTK&#39;s intelligence is unknown. Alluding the police for decades, especially police that share very little or none of the important BTK communications with the public, does not qualify BTK as above intelligent.  <BR> <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 1876-->
<!--Post: 1889--><!--Time: 1103076804-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST1889"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Ivy_leaguer<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Ivy_leaguer<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->10-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, December 14, 2004 - 6:13 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->The name BTK is a recent development. Between 1974 and 2003 the unknown subject was known as the BTK Strangler. He crafted the sobriquet in any early letter to the police. View any publication during the last thirty years examining the Wichita serial murders and the serial murderer is referred to as the BTK Strangler. <BR> <BR>The BTK Strangler is very intelligent. The planning involved in the crimes and the lengthy time spent at the crime scenes viewing the victims last breath, photographing the corpses and not leaving physical evidence at the crime scenes would suggest the unknown subject is an organized murderer. Organized murderers or serial murderers tend to be highly intelligent, meticulous and careful. Some people would suggest avoiding apprehension for nearly 31 years requires a high degree of intelligence. Not many prominent serial murderers have achieved it.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 1889-->
<!--Post: 1893--><!--Time: 1103078994-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST1893"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><a href="mailto:raolesin@hotmail.com" target="_blank"><!--/email--><!--name-->Ryan_olesin<!--/name--></b></a>
</a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Ryan_olesin<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->5-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, December 14, 2004 - 6:49 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Interesting similarity. <BR> <BR>BTK&#39;s 13th chapter is &#34;Will there be more&#34; <BR>The Bates letters in the Zodiac case said &#34;There will be more&#34;<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 1893-->
<!--Post: 2138--><!--Time: 1103336656-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST2138"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Tac<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Tac<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->11-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, December 17, 2004 - 6:24 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Ivy, <BR> <BR>Many of the highly publicized serial killers like the Zodiac, Bundy were thought &#40;proven in Bundy&#39;s case&#41; to be highly intelligent. But for every Bundy or Zodiac there is a Henry Lee Lucas, Ed Kemper, Green River Killer or Gaci and many lesser publicized men. I just don&#39;t buy it that all or the majority of serial killers are highly intelligent even if they have eluded capture. <BR> <BR>Remember the concept or definition created for these people, Serial Killer, is a relatively new label. I think we need more case study and more comparison but that&#39;s just my opinion. <BR> <BR>I stand corrected indeed the orginal references did include &#34;strangler&#34; as part of the BTK name. <BR> <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 2138-->
<!--Post: 2139--><!--Time: 1103336938-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST2139"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Tac<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Tac<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->11-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, December 17, 2004 - 6:28 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Ryan, <BR> <BR>Great observation and it seems to fit that BTK would attempted to inject Zodiac references in the most subtle ways possible.  .  .  <!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 2139-->
<!--Post: 2172--><!--Time: 1103360419-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST2172"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Ivy_leaguer<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Ivy_leaguer<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->10-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, December 18, 2004 - 1:00 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Not all serial murderers are highly intelligent or intelligent. Some possess low intelligence, such as Gary Ridgway. Ted Bundy was considered to have above average intelligence with an IQ of 124. Michael Ross, a Cornell graduate has an IQ of 122. Big Ed Kemper is considered to be highly intelligent with an IQ of 140 and greater. Possessing a high IQ does not imply a serial murderer is intelligent. Competence, sophistication and complexity in manipulation, control and domination determines the serial murderers&#39; level of intelligence. <BR>    <BR>Psychological profilers have suggested the BTK Strangler is highly intelligent in terms of academic achievement and competence. Michael Kelleher believes the BTK Strangler studied engineering or the physical sciences at an undergraduate level at college. Dr. Deborah Schurman-Kauflin from the Violent Crimes Institute prepared a psychological portrait of the unknown subject in 2000 and determined the BTK Strangler is highly intelligent. Her assessment was based on his desire to read books on true crime, poetry and literature. She believes he is capable of concealing his psychopathic traits of control and domination with a facade of modesty and conservatism.   <BR> <BR>The term &#39;serial killer&#39; was devised by Robert K. Ressler from the FBI in 1976. Serial murderers are different. Their fantasies and motives are distinct. Some may use the same modus operandi, some may use a signature and others do not. Studies have demonstrated the &#39;typical&#39; serial murderer does not exist.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 2172-->
<!--Post: 2222--><!--Time: 1103425427-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST2222"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Tac<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Tac<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->11-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, December 18, 2004 - 7:03 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Ivy, <BR> <BR>Facinating.  .  .  can I assume then that a serial killer&#39;s motives and fantasies may be random and spur of the moment ideas produced by their impulses ? Would this make the serial killer a easier person to catch because they might strike out without planning their attacks ? <BR> <BR>If the serial murderer is more distinctive in his fantasies and motives might this person be more thorough in planning and executing his crime ? This person then would fit the BTK and Zodiac mold ? <!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 2222-->
<!--Post: 2243--><!--Time: 1103443982-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST2243"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Ivy_leaguer<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Ivy_leaguer<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->10-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Sunday, December 19, 2004 - 12:13 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Ed Kemper&#39;s motive and fantasies were different to Ted Bundy, John Wayne Gacy and others. Kemper murdered coeds to counter his mother&#39;s claim they were too good for him. He was motivated by hatred for his mother. Ted Bundy&#39;s motives were more complex. In essence, Bundy murdered women who reminded him of Stephanie Brooks, the girlfriend who rejected him. He thought she was the epitome of refinement, breeding and bourgeois values and was devastated by the dissolution of the relationship. Some serial murderers commit murder on impulse, some are more restrained and disciplined. It does not imply the perpetrator who acted on impulse will be easier to apprehend, but its more likely. <BR> <BR>The BTK Strangler and the Zodiac Killer are considered organized serial murderers. They are more meticulous and careful in planning and executing a murder. They are not likely to commit a murder based on impulse. They are likely to select a victim, survey them, plan an attack and then commit murder. <!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 2243-->
<!--Post: 2245--><!--Time: 1103444837-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST2245"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Steve_huff<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Steve_huff<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Sunday, December 19, 2004 - 12:27 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Ivy,  <BR> <BR>While I know you are correct about Kemper and Bundy, I don&#39;t think you are including the caveat that both were also sadistic psychopaths who found they got a sexual charge out of the pain and death of others, too - in Kemper&#39;s case, actually, some of the adolescent games he played with his sister seem to indicate he was a bit like Albert Fish, and liked the idea of his own pain and death too...that said, I&#39;m not disagreeing, just adding.  <BR> <BR>As to BTK/Z, I agree completely that this was obviously their methodolog&#40;ies&#41; - but I still am puzzling what bottomline trigger got pulled that sent either BTK or Zodiac into the surveillance/stalking/killing cycle. As you pointed out, Kemper had his Mother, and Bundy had Stephanie Brooks to stand there as a symbol of what he wanted to possess, eradicate - what dynamic kicked in with BTK? If we look solely at the women BTK killed, all save Shirley Vian and Julie Otero have a certain look to them, to my eyes. Nancy Fox was a handsome woman, didn&#39;t look to be very big, Vicki Wegerle appears to have been slight and girlish, and it&#39;s possible Kathryn Bright was as well. Could it be that with BTK there was a mix of such motives? If these victims were representing &#40;a la Bundy/Brooks&#41; someone, who? And then there&#39;s this - could there have been something different motivating the Otero murders entirely?  <BR> <BR>It&#39;s a relief to go ahead and post here after lurking for quite a bit. Posters like Ivy Leaguer, Victor &#40;VSCantu&#41; and others are an intellectual breath of fresh air after some of the other forums I&#39;ve read. <BR> <BR>Steve<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 2245-->
<!--Post: 2256--><!--Time: 1103477370-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST2256"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Mike_kelleher<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Mike_kelleher<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->5-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Sunday, December 19, 2004 - 9:29 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Very interesting discussion. Thanks. I would point out only one detail to keep in mind as the thread continues. All serial killers exhibit both MO and signature. The MO can change, and often does. Sometimes this is a conscious decision on the part of the killer, sometimes not. The signature will not change. That is an unconscious or semi-conscious part of the crime that provides the best behavioral indicators. <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 2256-->
<!--Post: 2279--><!--Time: 1103493218-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST2279"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Ivy_leaguer<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Ivy_leaguer<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->10-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Sunday, December 19, 2004 - 1:53 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->There is no doubt Kemper and Bundy were sadistic psychopaths who enjoyed inflicting pain and suffering on their victims. They derived sexual gratification from viewing the suffering of victims. Bundy mentioned he enjoyed observing the last breath of his victims. <BR> <BR>It seems the Zodiac Killer could have been motivated by rejection or the inability to have an intimate relationship with women. He attacked three couples enjoying a date. His anger and frustration from adolescence increased during adulthood. His lack of success with women may be attributed to them not considering him physically or sexually desirable or he was asocial. After years of frustration he reached the conclusion experiencing sexual gratification was more likely to occur through murder than a romantic relationship with a woman. <BR> <BR>Violent sexual fantasies are at the foundation of the BTK Strangler&#39;s motivation. The unknown subject would consider Nancy Fox and Kathryn Bright and Vicki Wegerle to be attractive and realized he could only have a relationship with them in death. The murder of Shirley Vian may suggest the type of woman that reminds him of his mother or wife or  former girlfriends or all of them. Julie Otero may be included in this category. The BTK Strangler wanted Vian and Otero to experience the suffering women in his life caused him. <BR> <BR>I will amend an earlier statement concerning signature. Modus operandi is dynamic and may change. A serial murderer tends to improve and refine the modus operandi during their period of operation. Signature is static and reveals the perpetrator&#39;s personal expression. However, a signature may evolve over time where a sexually sadistic murderer performs more postmortem mutilation from one murder to another. In some cases the police may have diffculty determining the signature due to decomposition of the corpse or interruptions of the murderer&#39;s routine.     <!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 2279-->
<!--Post: 2281--><!--Time: 1103494001-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST2281"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Oliver<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Oliver<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->10-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Sunday, December 19, 2004 - 2:06 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Has it ever occurred to you that BTK could have just developed the way he is. With murder/torture his only way of sexual gratification. Maybe he doesn&#39;t even know why. Maybe it had nothing to do with his environment or the way he grew up.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 2281-->
<!--Post: 2282--><!--Time: 1103494784-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST2282"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><a href="mailto:webmaster@catchbtk.com" target="_blank"><!--/email--><!--name-->Tom_voigt<!--/name--></b></a>
</a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Tom_voigt<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->5-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Sunday, December 19, 2004 - 2:19 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Oliver, this discussion is about the similarities between BTK and the Zodiac.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 2282-->
<!--Post: 2414--><!--Time: 1103615303-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST2414"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><a href="mailto:vscantu1@msn.com" target="_blank"><!--/email--><!--name-->Vscantu<!--/name--></b></a>
</a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Vscantu<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Monday, December 20, 2004 - 11:48 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I have to at least partly agree with Oliver: BTK and Zodiac both could easily have become serial killers just &#34;out of the blue&#34;. Kinda like Bundy, who was an upstanding yuppie law-school student. Sure, he might of had a lousey breadup with a girlfriend, but who among us hasn&#39;t? Who among us hasn&#39;t? <BR> <BR>In other words, BTK &amp; Zodiac may be otherwise &#34;normal&#34; persons in society. Not hideous-looking gargoyles with green skin &amp; horns sticking out of their heads. In fact, this totally &#34;normal&#34; appearence is exactly what may have kept them from ever being noticed or captured during their respective lifetimes. <BR> <BR>But that could change soon.....<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 2414-->
<!--Post: 2417--><!--Time: 1103621837-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST2417"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Steve_huff<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Steve_huff<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, December 21, 2004 - 1:37 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I could not have said this any better, Victor - I think one of the big commonalities BTK and Zodiac may have will be this kind of bland-seeming, nebbishy camouflage of a personality. They will seem as inadequate to casual acquaintances as they are, and will have learned to hide the pathologies early in life. They are both very innocuous men, who probably seem particularly harmless to relatives and/or acquaintances. This demeanor both contributed to their continuing to feel less than adequate, but also became their mask. <BR> <BR>Steve<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 2417-->
<!--Post: 2419--><!--Time: 1103624498-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST2419"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Ivy_leaguer<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Ivy_leaguer<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->10-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, December 21, 2004 - 2:21 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->The BTK Strangler and the Zodiac Killer would appear normal to relatives, acquaintances and people who met them in the community. The facade of respectability or the appearance of normality is a factor in both perpetrators avoiding punishment. The families of the BTK Strangler and the Zodiac Killer would be shocked and in a state of disbelief if they were captured. <BR> <BR>It would be unlikely the BTK Strangler and the Zodiac Killer suddenly decided to become serial murderers. Years of frustration, anger and inadequacy would have accumulated before the decision was made to murder and continue to murder. Both took enormous risks in murdering some of their victims. An individual who accepted such risks would expect a payoff that provided a high level of gratification. Murdering for sexual gratification could be justified by the BTK Strangler and Zodiac Killer. It would provide the motivation to murder for several years and most probably murder more victims that remain unknown. Murdering someone for fun would provide satisfaction in the short term, but not after murdering the sixth or seventh victim. The fun would dissipate. The economic principle of the law of diminishing returns would apply. The murderer would believe the risks surpassed the gratification derived and would contemplate not murdering in the future or not murder in the future. The compulsion for the BTK Strangler and the Zodiac Killer to continue to murder would be greater than murdering for fun. Psychopaths murder for enjoyment and to assert control and dominance over the victim and realize their fantasies.  <!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 2419-->
<!--Post: 2625--><!--Time: 1103852057-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST2625"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Johno<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Johno<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, December 23, 2004 - 5:34 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Besides the obvious similarities between BKT and Zodiac I am curious as to peculiar verbage used by both. One word that one used and another word that the other used in a communique. For the Zodiac the trail is now too cold to link anyone who used his word and for BTK his word is now so common that no one could be linked to it&#39;s usage. <BR>Zodiac used the phrase &#39;airplane cement.&#39; Who would have called airplane glue airplane cement? I know that&#39;s probably what it said on the tube but everybody called it &#39;glue&#39; not &#39;cement.&#39; It&#39;s almost like Zodiac was reading that off the tube when he wrote the letter of which he refered to it. I used airplane glue to make models throughout the decade of the 60&#39;s and neither myself nor anyone I knew ever called it airplane cement. It was always airplane glue. Even today over on Tom&#39;s Zodiac site there is a thread on the message board called &#39;Airplane Glue Fingerprints&#39; and neither the poster or the 19 respondants to the thread refer to Zodiacs original word of &#39;cement.&#39; They all call it &#39;glue.&#39;  <BR>If I had been a detective at the time I would have canvassed Vallejo area stores that sold it and asked if anyone was asking for airplane cement as opposed to the general public&#39;s use of the word glue. During the time of the letter in which he refers to it, gluesniffing was becomeing so popular that most stores kept it behind the counter to keep people from shoplifting it so you had to ask for it. <BR>BTK uses the word &#39;dude&#39; where he obviously ment to use it&#39;s plural form of dudes. How large of a segment of the general population of the USA or Wichita used the word &#39;dude&#39; in everyday use in 1974? <BR>The word had been around since it&#39;s introduction in New York in the 1870&#39;s to mean a &#39;Dandy&#39; and so in the 1920&#39;s, 30&#39;s and 40&#39;s someone who was from the east who was out west was a &#39;Dude.&#39; The surfers in California first used it in about 1960 to mean a wimp and by the decade&#39;s end it was any male. In the 1970&#39;s it came to mean by many, a black male as in a black dude instead of a black guy. Blacks and whites alike in the deep south often used &#39;dude&#39; in the 70&#39;s to refer to any male of any race. The song &#34;All the Young Dudes&#34; came out in 1972 but the dude didn&#39;t filter down as a commonly used word by the American listeners of that song. In March of 1974, seven months before BTK used the word in his October &#39;74 letter, Steely Dan released an album with the song &#34;Any Major Dude Will Tell You&#34; but still the word was not in all that common everyday usage. I was in college in 1974 and I don&#39;t remember many people using it in everyday slang. <BR>Why would Zodiac refer to Airplane Cement as it&#39;s actual label name and not the more commonly used airplane glue? <BR>Why would a 25-30 year old BTK use the word dude?<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 2625-->
<!--Post: 2665--><!--Time: 1103897748-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST2665"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Juno<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Juno<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->5-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, December 24, 2004 - 6:15 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Airplane cement wouldbe called &#34;cement&#34; by a hobbyist, person who works in a place where they sell it &#40;hobby shop or hobby department in store&#41;. <BR> <BR>It may also be called cement by someone who read about it&#39;s other uses in a book which referred to it as &#34;cement.&#34; <BR> <BR>Most people whose initial awareness of it was as &#34;cement&#34; will refer to it as cement. <BR> <BR> <BR> <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 2665-->
<!--Post: 2666--><!--Time: 1103898156-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST2666"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Juno<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Juno<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->5-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, December 24, 2004 - 6:22 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Dude!   <BR>You forgot the counter-cuture! <BR> <BR>1969--Easy Rider: they used &#34;dude&#34; a lot throughout the movie.  So frequently that one character asks about it. <BR> <BR>George Hanson: What&#39;s &#34;dude&#34;? Is that like &#34;dude ranch&#34;? <BR>Captain America: Dude means nice guy. Dude means a regular sort of person. <BR> <BR>Maybe the George Hanson&#39;s weren&#39;t using the word, but in any college town i&#39;m sure the word was well known by the mid seventies.  I remember saying it as a kid all the time around 74 or 75 and my mom thought it was &#34;bad&#34; and didn&#39;t want us using it. <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 2666-->
<!--Post: 2667--><!--Time: 1103899017-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST2667"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Juno<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Juno<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->5-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, December 24, 2004 - 6:36 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->BTK and Zodiac both &#34;had a nasty reputation as a cru-el dude&#34;  <BR> <BR>&#34;Life in the fast lane&#34;--1976 <BR> <BR> <BR> <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 2667-->
<!--Post: 2676--><!--Time: 1103913281-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST2676"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><a href="mailto:vscantu1@msn.com" target="_blank"><!--/email--><!--name-->Vscantu<!--/name--></b></a>
</a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Vscantu<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, December 24, 2004 - 10:34 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I THINK WE CAN NOW PUT THE &#34;DUDE&#34; QUESTION SAFELY TO REST. Thank You. <BR> <BR>Victor<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 2676-->
<!--Post: 2694--><!--Time: 1103944777-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST2694"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Johno<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Johno<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, December 24, 2004 - 7:19 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->All valid points made and thanks to Juno and Vscantu for the input but not to rest the DUDE just yet. Kids and Bikers and Rockers all true but a man between 25 and 30 as BTK&#39;s age in 1974 has been estimated or 35 in 1974 if you go by his questionable self biography? Would someone in that age group be using the word &#39;dude&#39;? Maybe it pinpoints him to being under 25 years old in 1974. Early 20&#39;s? Biker? Surfer? Hippy? Rocker? <BR>How about Zodiac, was he a hobbiest and built model cars and airplanes etc?<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 2694-->
<!--Post: 2699--><!--Time: 1103954009-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST2699"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Ivy_leaguer<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Ivy_leaguer<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->10-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, December 24, 2004 - 9:53 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->The term &#39;dude&#39; was an attempt to convince the police and the public the unknown subject was young and possibly between 18 and 25 in the mid 1970s. It could allow the BTK Strangler to be associated with an alternative lifestyle and culture questioning the values and legitimacy of bourgeois society. It could be genuine or more likely an attempt to manipulate the police and the public.  <BR> <BR>The Zodiac Killer most probably enjoyed building model airplanes and cars. The reference to &#39;airplane cement&#39; could indicate he used it for another purpose. A purpose that requires more investigation on my part. <!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 2699-->
<!--Post: 3074--><!--Time: 1104427183-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST3074"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><a href="mailto:dammitslicky@cox.net" target="_blank"><!--/email--><!--name-->Cougar<!--/name--></b></a>
</a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Cougar<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->10-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, December 30, 2004 - 9:19 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I am no Zodiac expert. But didn&#39;t at least one of his crimes occur in an area called Riverside? And if so, I wonder if this is why he picked Vickie Wegerle - OR, worse yet, I wonder if he plans a future crime in the Riverside area of Wichita. Wegerle lived just west and north of Riverside, although some might confuse the two. She actually lived in Indian Hills. <!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 3074-->
<!--Post: 3100--><!--Time: 1104451525-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST3100"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Koolaid<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Koolaid<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, December 30, 2004 - 4:05 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I live in Riverside, in fact I&#39;m sitting in my office two blocks from the murder location, I went to school there also. I&#39;ve read the reports on the Bates murder and other then the letters mailed to our Police department and the press there is no real evidence that the Zodiac did the crime. It was my understanding that the Zodiac loved attention and took credit for the crime he did not commit. Her boyfriend was the prime suspect, and there were witnesses to heated arguments between the two. The police had an informant in the jail that claimed her boyfriend bragged of the incident. All of Zodiacs murders took place in Northern California, why would he come all the way down here for one? Wierd you mention Indian Hills, just north-east of Riverside, Ca. is a small community called Indian Hills.  <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 3100-->
<!--Post: 3101--><!--Time: 1104451767-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST3101"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><a href="mailto:dammitslicky@cox.net" target="_blank"><!--/email--><!--name-->Cougar<!--/name--></b></a>
</a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Cougar<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->10-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, December 30, 2004 - 4:09 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->That&#39;s freaky, koolaid. Really freaky. I think Those who live in the Riverside area of Wichita should take note of this. I know I am. <!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 3101-->
<!--Post: 3269--><!--Time: 1104707833-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST3269"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Sandy<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Sandy<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->5-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Sunday, January 02, 2005 - 3:17 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Tac, You are correct,that is the same guy.  I was told Kane did work for the mob, and was a hit man for them .I would like to know if Allen Dorphman was killed in Kanas, and what was the date ?<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 3269-->
<!--Post: 3271--><!--Time: 1104709059-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST3271"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Sandy<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Sandy<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->5-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Sunday, January 02, 2005 - 3:37 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Koolaid, How do you know all of the Zodiacs victims were only in the north part of Calif ? If the Man Darleen was most afraid of was in fact the Z ,she herself said: He must have come back, from out of state. This tells me he could have victims anywhere.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 3271-->
<!--Post: 3304--><!--Time: 1104771007-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST3304"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Koolaid<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Koolaid<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Monday, January 03, 2005 - 8:50 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I was speaking of known victims. <!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 3304-->
<!--Post: 3355--><!--Time: 1104819915-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST3355"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->George<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->George<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->7-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Monday, January 03, 2005 - 10:25 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I&#39;ve read that Kane has, among a slew of other extraordianry gifts, an IQ of 170, that he can change his appearance drastically at the drop of a hat &#40;one half of his face has been paralyzed for years&#41; and now he was a hitman for the mob. I have my doubts whether or not Kane was the Zodiac but the point here is that I wouldn&#39;t loosely accuse him of being a hit man for an organized crime family -- in other words, a murderer beyond the Zodiac crimes -- without documentation to prove it. Or maybe Tom doesn&#39;t fear lawsuits? <BR> <BR>Koolaid: I like your approach.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 3355-->
<!--Post: 3367--><!--Time: 1104829849-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST3367"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->George<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->George<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->7-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, January 04, 2005 - 1:10 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->&#34;This is the Zodiac speaking,&#34; announces the self-named Zodiac. BTK, whether seriously or not, does <BR>&#34;suggest&#34; that the media decide what to call him and offers nine suggestions. Three of the nine have &#34;Wichita&#34; in the title. To me, this indicates that BTK will be happy enough with Wichita acknowledging his existence. Zodiac needed millions to know who he was, and fired off one letter to Los Angeles, claiming that the SF press was burying him in the back pages. <BR> <BR>Another example that both killers wanted press coverage but that Zodiac needed it much more than BTK. That is, until nearly ten months ago. Like Zodiac after the Stine murder, BTK is peppering Wichita media. Like Zodiac after Stine, BTK only has words to play with.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 3367-->
<!--Post: 4209--><!--Time: 1105872941-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST4209"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Boffa994<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Boffa994<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->1-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Sunday, January 16, 2005 - 2:55 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Hey BTK are you so pathetic you cant come up with anything  original of your own. Hate to break it to you, but you aren&#39;t even in the Zodiac&#39;s league. His cyphers are more complicated and more thought oriented then yours will ever be. Your outclassed and outmatched. Zodiac even had more balls when communicating with the police and media taking even more risks. Your just a second rate copycat. I cant believe most people on this site and other sites think you could possibly be Zodiac or that your really intelligent. Your just lucky. I think Toms right. If your ever caught people are going to be very suprised that your not that bright of an individual like gary ridgeway, the green river killer.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 4209-->
<!--Post: 5995--><!--Time: 1107476035-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST5995"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Lmuncy316<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Lmuncy316<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, February 03, 2005 - 4:13 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->If I&#39;m not mistaken there was a 2 1/2 year gap between zodiac&#39;s letters at one point and time, and, if I&#39;m not mistaken, there was a two-year five-month gap between BTK&#39;s Bright murder and letter taking claim for the Otero murders.  Maybe just another subtle way of attempting to adapt zodiac&#39;s into his own murders.  &#40;Copycatting&#41;<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 5995-->
<!--Post: 7527--><!--Time: 1108560678-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST7527"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Embersage<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Embersage<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Wednesday, February 16, 2005 - 5:31 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I think the biggest problem at the end of the 60&#39;s and early part of the 70&#39;s was the fact that men were coming home from war. I was a kid then and I think there were a few of these guys who could not stop killing, that is way there are so many unsolved murders at that time. I believe that BTK followed Zodiac&#39;s MO in the beginning until he felt comfortable with his own MO. If your going to follow the pattern of a killer, should be one that wasn&#39;t caught. I have told Tom about a Guy in Washington State who after 20 years was just recently convicted of binding raping,beating, and killing a girl in 1976, He was extremely violent and no one knows what he has been doing the past 20 years, I can tell you he has family in Kansas, No I don&#39;t think it&#39;s BTK but they have the same Type of MO, around the same age.  The difference between Zodiac and BTK is that Zodiac basically hates people, and BTK had an aggressive,primial drive to release sexual tension. The did some of the same things, but for different reasons and that is how you tell them apart. <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 7527-->
<!--Post: 12510--><!--Time: 1112325443-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12510"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Ivy_leaguer<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Ivy_leaguer<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->10-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, March 31, 2005 - 7:17 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->There are several similarities between the BTK Strangler and the Zodiac Killer. Both committed murder to satisfy violent sexual fantasies. The BTK Strangler bound most of his victims before strangling them, while the Zodiac primarily approached couples in an intimate setting. They corresponded with the media and law enforcement taunting them with puzzles and providing details of their victims.  <BR> <BR>Do the similarities extend to the capture of the Zodiac Killer? Some believe he is dead, while others believe he may be frail and elderly. I believe there is a high probability the unknown subject is alive. Michael Kelleher in &#34;This is the Zodiac Speaking&#34;: Into the Mind of a Serial Killer suggested the perpetrator may be born in the late 1930s or early 1940s. I agree with this assessment. In 2005, the perpetrator would be in his early to late 60s. He most probably is living in northern California with the belief he will never be captured. He feels comfortable in an environment he claimed his victims and the belief held by the public and some police the Zodiac is most likely deceased or was Arthur Leigh Allen. A campaign suggesting the Zodiac is alive and living among the free may change his attitude. The unknown subject may react differently today compared to 36 years ago when he is placed under the microscope.      <!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12510-->
<!--Post: 12511--><!--Time: 1112329135-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12511"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Steve_huff<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Steve_huff<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, March 31, 2005 - 8:18 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Ivy_leaguer, I think that&#39;s a fantastic idea.  <BR> <BR>I am not even convinced that Zodiac is not still killing. Tom already caught onto the striking similarities between Zodiac-style murders and the murders last August of Lindsey Cutshall and Jason Allen on a beach near Sonoma.  <BR> <BR>While I was writing a blog entry on it, I realized another mysterious double murder had taken place in Napa, not far from that beach, in November 2004 - this of two women, Leslie Mazzara and Adriane Insogna, <i>by knife.</i> The timing of Mazzara and Insogna&#39;s murders was even perhaps Zodiac-like; early a.m. Nov. 1 - basically Halloween night. This timing may also indicate a less mature killer striving to amplify the brutality, the &#34;spookiness&#34; of his crime, but the close proximity to Sonoma as well as being in Zodiac&#39;s old stomping grounds was striking, to me. Here is the link to that blog entry; <a href="http://www.planethuff.com/darkside/archives/000633.html#trackbacks"><i>Why Not Zodiac?</i></a> <BR> <BR>Perhaps Zodiac has moved past the game-playing of 30 years ago and has decided that for a serial killer, actions speak louder than words. I also think it&#39;s feasible to think Zodiac could even be the same age as BTK - say Cheri Jo Bates was the first Zodiac murder &#40;1966&#41;, he still would have been in his 20&#39;s. <!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12511-->
<!--Post: 12516--><!--Time: 1112335789-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12516"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Ivy_leaguer<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Ivy_leaguer<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->10-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, March 31, 2005 - 10:09 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Steve, <BR> <BR>I read your blog and found the analysis to be superb. The modus operandi of the murders of Jason Allen and Lindsay Cutshall is similar to the modus operandi used by the Zodiac. Serial murderers only stop murdering when they are incarcerated, severely ill or dead. I believe the Zodiac claimed more victims than is currently attributed to him. He most probably ceased communicating with the media and police to avoid apprehension. Its possible he committed the murders in the latter part of 2004 to demonstrate the BTK Strangler was not the only serial murderer enjoying freedom.  <!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12516-->
<!--Post: 12518--><!--Time: 1112339571-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12518"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Embersage<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Embersage<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, March 31, 2005 - 11:12 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Steve and Ivy_leaguer,  <BR>So here we are, The idea of a killer stoping, we have always been taught that 1.they were dead 2. In Jail 3.very ill. Now we know with Rader that was not the case. Zodiac, believe was also hard at work, you both know a lot more about these cases than i do, so i would like to ask a question, Didn&#39;t Bundy start kiliing the same time Rader did? DID someone start a club, we dont know about. I know Lieut. Col. Michael Aquino &#40;looks like dracula lol&#41; was running the Church of satanism from 1981-1986 in SF. The church wasnt a big deal when started by LaVey, but it took on a whole new meaning under Aquino, yes i have extensive knowledge of the church. Thanks E.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12518-->
<!--Post: 12519--><!--Time: 1112345735-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12519"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Tac<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Tac<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->11-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, April 01, 2005 - 12:55 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Ivy, <BR> <BR>What Microscope ? SFPD abandoned the case last year. Parts of the original evidence has either been lost or stolen.  .  .  <BR> <BR>There is absolutely no piece of forensic evidence that can be positively 100% varified as the Zodiac&#39;s dna.  .  .  <BR> <BR>Couple this with no official LE agency searching or putting pressure on and you have what amounts to NO microscope.  .  . <!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12519-->
<!--Post: 12520--><!--Time: 1112346366-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12520"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Steve_huff<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Steve_huff<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, April 01, 2005 - 1:06 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Embersage; As tempting as it is to wonder about such a dark and awful conspiracy, I don&#39;t think there was a club involved. The very nature of the psychopathic serial killer mitigates against it. They don&#39;t bond - with each other, even. There&#39;s the funny story of a number of the more famous ones getting together in a California lockup to play cards - probably one helluva stage play in that scenario - but that&#39;s after they were caught, and even then, be assured they all cheated and lied their butts off with abandon. They are rarely the kind to hold an allegiance with others, much less the type to put up with any kind of leadership structure, such as a group like that would need.  <BR> <BR>What was at work in the recent past was a manifold number of things; the explosion of the press, for instance. Consider that by the end of the 70&#39;s most newspapers were using laserphoto, an early fax-type system. Photos of events and people could be sent out to papers everywhere, and fast. CNN came on air in 1980 or 81, further nationalizing, then globalizing news coverage. Also, the country as a whole underwent a sea change in culture that really is astonishing in retrospect. Certain events symbolize the changes - the Kennedy assassination; a loss of innocence, a sense of betrayal and disappointment. The Vietnam era - more of the same. Then the hippie movement, founded with it&#39;s roots in freedom of expression, among other things, has it&#39;s Walpurgis Nacht in Woodstock and it&#39;s All Hallow&#39;s Eve all at once in early August of 1969, when the Manson family also did it&#39;s bloody deeds.  Roll into America the horny, the 70&#39;s, with free love, drug experimentation, still aquifers of influence - liberation the keyword of the times. Serial Killers were truly born. Yes, they&#39;d always been here. They were like LL Cool J that way, it wasn&#39;t really a comeback - but what happened was we <i>saw</i> them. The culture at large had been like the blind men feeling the parts of the elephant, trying to describe the beast, and the collusion of culture and worldwide news coverage being sped by technology resulted in what looked like a &#34;serial killer explosion.&#34;  <BR> <BR>Key phrase, <i>&#34;looked like&#34;</i>. That&#39;s my point, I guess - they were always here. And what happened is others witnessed the infamy garnered by killers like Zodiac, like Bundy, and seeing this fed into their own lust not just for power over life and death, but <i>fame</i>. Rader will be shown to have been one of these who saw this happen, who lusted for the fame. It&#39;s obvious, really, from his aping in some instances of Zodiac&#39;s &#34;style.&#34; It&#39;s interesting that he&#39;s now been caught, because his crimes and the way he publicized themselves are so very characteristic of a seventies-era serial. It&#39;s like catching a coelocanth, because I personally thought that breed of serial had passed, and the ones at work today are the silent movers, the suspect zeros quietly killing in every town and moving on. <BR> <BR>It wasn&#39;t that the initial infamy acquired by the first killers <i>caused</i> the actions of the others, it was that it may have given a mind like BTK/Rader&#39;s a sense of &#34;permission&#34; to go ahead with what he&#39;d been wanting to do since very early in life.  <BR> <BR>Embersage, if there is a conspiracy, it&#39;s an unwitting one entered into by the public - us - and the killer themselves. It&#39;s the conspiracy that makes great movies like <i>Silence of the Lambs</i>, or long before that, <i>Psycho</i>. This does not mean us non-killers need to serve time or anything; it does mean we need to keep calling these animals what they are, and not feed into their ultimate desire for a kind of omnipotence. FBI agents know this - if you read books by any former profiler, you hear it in their language. They refuse to accord serials the power they know the murderer ultimately hungers for and call them what they are; monsters. And true monsters are not superior to us; they are vermin.  <BR> <BR>That&#39;s the conspiracy; the one that gives the killer the sense of power he wants. The internet allows it, explodes it really. But what is one to do? We <i>need</i> info like you find here and at ZodiacKiller.com, or even on my blog - it&#39;s necessary not to give the killer some kind of ego-high, but to <i>warn others, and warn the future.</i> <BR> <BR>I think taking their power away while not being silent starts with taking away the veneer of mysticism. The idea some Satanic conspiracy might be behind all these guys adds to that mysticism. Look at BTK; he was the Phantom, the Wichita Hangman, the boogeyman. Now we see he was a nebbishy, petty little bureaucrat whose outward reputation was that of a hypocrite and a busybody. Yes, he was a shadow all these years, but the joke&#39;s on us - his true self is the nebbish, and where other creeps have bad secrets like wiping their snot on every public conveyance&#39;s seating, Rader&#39;s bad secret was just the worst possible to conceive of. I can guarantee you that should we ever find out who Zodiac was, he will be similar to Rader this way. A very unremarkable man. A dork, who liked G&amp;S and was obsessed with things nautical. They are all this way, and the killing is the only thing that gives them a sense that they are more than human garbage. It&#39;s an inverted psychology that normal people will never truly understand.  <BR> <BR>That&#39;s why I call him Denny the Dog Catcher. Names are power - the ancients knew that; in the opera <i>Lohengrin</i> by Richard Wagner, the crime that undoes the marital bliss of the hero knight and his lady Elsa of Brabant is her asking him his true name. Wagner took his libretto from very old norse myths and stories, showing how archaic the idea of the power of a name truly is. Thing is, that hasn&#39;t truly vanished from our psyche. When Rader was BTK he was a creature of power. Now that we know he&#39;s Denny the Dog Catcher, a sour-faced man with bad hair and a pissy mien, he seems far less scary than we could have ever imagined, doesn&#39;t he? If I ever find out Zodiac was really, say, Dave the database admin, I guarantee you he will cease to be Zodiac and become nasty old Dave, to me.  <BR> <BR>Hope that all makes sense.   <BR> <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12520-->
<!--Post: 12521--><!--Time: 1112346799-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12521"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Steve_huff<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Steve_huff<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, April 01, 2005 - 1:13 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Ivy; <BR> <BR>That&#39;s doubly high praise about my analysis, coming from <u>you</u> - I thank you, very much. I&#39;m going to write some more about it soon, especially since I&#39;ve studied the Mazzara/Insogna murder a little more.  <BR> <BR>I truly am mystified, by the way, that AMW would broadcast the idea that one killer who vanished from Canada 30 years ago might be connected when a killer from Northern California with the same MO, who also preferred .45 caliber weapons, is just as likely to be the culprit. I mean, does LE and AMW really just assume Zodiac is dead? I&#39;m with you on that, Ivy_leaguer. There&#39;s no reason to believe he did anything but realize that his communicating with police might just screw him eventually. If nothing else, Denny the Dog Catcher&#39;s purple diskette o&#39; death has reinforced that lesson for our boy Zodiac. <!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12521-->
<!--Post: 12522--><!--Time: 1112360518-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12522"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Embersage<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Embersage<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, April 01, 2005 - 5:01 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Thank you Steve and a quote to reinforce your words from John Douglas. &#34;The Cases That Haunt Us.&#34; Chapter 4 The Zodiac. paragraph One. <BR> <BR>&#34;One thing that motivates many serial offenders is the desire to create and sustain their own Mythology.&#34;  <BR> <BR>paragraph two <BR>&#34;The reasons they feel the need to do this are obvious to those of us in criminal investigation analysis.&#34; &#34;These are insignificant nobodies whose only &#34;accomplishment&#34; in life, the only time they feel in control and fulfilled, is when they are causing suffering or fear in others.&#34; <BR> <BR> <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12522-->
<!--Post: 12530--><!--Time: 1112373303-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12530"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Elsewhere<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Elsewhere<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->10-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, April 01, 2005 - 8:35 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I tell you what, all of you posting here from the 31 - 1st. should seriously consider writing a book...or forming a dective agency or SOMETHING.  Your insight is phenomonminal &#40;sp?&#41;...your writing and prestentation is penomoninal...it is so interesting to read what you have posted...so intelliegent <BR>I would buy your book! &#40;only if you promised to sign it<IMG SRC="http://www.catchbtk.com/discus/clipart/happy.gif" ALT=":-&#41;" BORDER=0> <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12530-->
<!--Post: 12535--><!--Time: 1112396419-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12535"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Ivy_leaguer<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Ivy_leaguer<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->10-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, April 01, 2005 - 3:00 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Embersage, <BR> <BR>There is sufficient evidence to substantiate the claim serial murderers will continue to murder unless they are severely ill, incarcerated or deceased. After Ted Bundy escaped from Garfield County Jail in December 1977, he murdered Lisa Levy, Margaret Bowman and Kimberly Leach. Gary Ridgway known as the Green River Killer admitted to murdering his last victim in 1998. He was arrested in 2001. The investigation into Dennis Rader is incomplete. Before March 2004, the police believed Nancy Fox was the BTK Strangler&#39;s last victim. In March 2004, the BTK Strangler resumed corresponding with the media and the Wichita Police Department and admitted to murdering Vicki Wegerle in 1986. In February 2005, Dennis Rader was arrested and charged with ten murders including Marine Hedge and Dolores Davis. The Wichita Police Department may discover more murders committed by Rader during the last 15 years. <BR> <BR>Tac, <BR> <BR>I am aware the San Francisco Police Department closed its invesitgation into the Zodiac murders in 2004. It will be difficult to apply forensic science to physical evidence from the late 1960s and early 1970s. The items may not be preserved and biological evidence is most likely degraded and the theft and disappearance of some items would hinder the investigation. I am suggesting a reversal in the belief Zodiac is deceased may prompt a response from the perpetrator. The perpetrator may begin to feel uncomfortable and no longer possess the discipline and restraint he once had to avoid apprehension.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12535-->
<!--Post: 12536--><!--Time: 1112397231-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12536"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Ivy_leaguer<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Ivy_leaguer<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->10-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, April 01, 2005 - 3:13 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Steve, <BR> <BR>I agree with you assessment of Joseph Burgess. It seems tenuous to suggest an American drifter who murdered a couple camping in the wilderness in British Columbia in 1972 would travel to northern California in 2004 and murder a couple.   <!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12536-->
<!--Post: 12539--><!--Time: 1112398255-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12539"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><a href="mailto:kathiemoffett@yahoo.com" target="_blank"><!--/email--><!--name-->Dahlia<!--/name--></b></a>
</a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Dahlia<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->3-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, April 01, 2005 - 3:30 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->All, <BR> <BR>As Ivy said, there is significant evidence that serials don&#39;t stop killing, under normal circumstances. However, Ted Bundy didn&#39;t live to be old. Hard to say what he&#39;d have done later in life, since from what I&#39;ve read there is also evidence that SOME serial killers do &#39;burn out&#39; or slow down as they age. Rader might be one of those; we don&#39;t know yet.  <BR> <BR>But obviously Z is another matter. I too have wondered about the timing &#40;and other aspects&#41; of the Napa murders and the Cutshall/Allen murders. If they are not Zodiac, they may well be a new killer on the scene, and he may well have been &#39;inspired&#39; by the return of BTK. I am very interested in the possibility that the two cases are connected. <BR> <BR>Steve, I believe the element of religious fanaticism is one reason LE is bringing up the Canadian loony. I think we must always bear in mind that LE undoubtedly knows something we don&#39;t, as they always keep so much back. So there may be some common element linking Cutshall/Allen to the Canadian murders that we simply don&#39;t know about. <BR> <BR>Ivy, you are aware, right, that there&#39;s been DNA testing in the Zodiac case? And indeed, the issues you mention have been raised--contamination of the samples, etc. And off-the-wall problems have cropped up as well: many folks&#39; favorite suspect, Arthur Leigh Allen, was posthumously tested and ruled out...but now we hear that apparently he hated licking stamps and had been known to make others do it for him &#40;usually kids...ick...he was a convicted molester&#41;.  <BR> <BR>I agree with Elsewhere--it&#39;s a pleasure to read these posts. Have you guys wandered around zodiackiller.com much? Both the new and archived message boards provide hours &#40;years?&#41; of fascinating reading and the site itself is simply encyclopedic. Dangerously so, lol. <BR> <BR>                          Dahlia <BR> <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12539-->
<!--Post: 12545--><!--Time: 1112404130-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12545"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Tac<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Tac<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->11-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, April 01, 2005 - 5:08 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Ivy, <BR> <BR>The &#34;exit&#34; interviews with SFPD mention that they thought the Zodiac may be alive. But the dept stance was that it was 50-50 at best that he was alive.  .  .  then they drop the case. <BR> <BR>That leaves no official LE agency running a case on the Zodiac. Without a official LE running a case, the media remains silent and disinterested. <BR> <BR>The only coverage is when Tom has a gathering on a Zodiac Anniversary. Those events get coverage in small local papers and a mention in SF rags.  .  .   <BR> <BR>But without publicity through media &#34;hype&#34; where is this campaign of pressure going to come from ? <BR> <BR>If you believe that all LE needs to do is announce that they have new evidence pointing to a living Z suspect I would have to disagree. Besides no LE is on the case to my knowledge just retired LE and us amatuers.  .  . <!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12545-->
<!--Post: 12550--><!--Time: 1112406539-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12550"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Steve_huff<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Steve_huff<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, April 01, 2005 - 5:48 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Dahlia, I agree with you about the religious fanaticism possibly dictating why Burgess was brought up as a suspect - and I do think it makes sense. My point is, Zodiac makes sense, too. Or, and if you ask me, this is the worst possibility, a copycat, as Tom V. seems to suspect. I keep waiting to hear that some major N. Cali newspaper or TV Station has received an odd letter about one of these murders. It wouldn&#39;t be the first time in the last 15 years or so that Zodiac spawned an imitator - remember Heriberto Seda, New York City&#39;s Zodiac from about a decade or more ago.  <BR> <BR>I&#39;m well aware it&#39;s part of LE standard operating procedure to hold info back - that&#39;s the only way they weed out false confessions and the like. But there&#39;s nothing wrong with my suggesting the possibility of a Zodiac connection, either, and I feel I&#39;ve supported my reasoning fairly well. To me, Zodiac&#39;s an equally good argument as Robert Burgess. Hell, it even occurred to me to wonder if Burgess <i>was</i> Zodiac, then it occurred to me that Burgess was a draft-dodger, and I believe that Zodiac was a navy man.  <BR> <BR>We are, as amateurs, only able to make arguments about such things based on the evidence that <i>is</i> available, and based on what we do know, I have a persistent feeling Z&#39;s lurking somewhere a bit north of his old stomping grounds.  <BR> <BR>Here&#39;s the thing; to me, BTK proved that some ghosts haven&#39;t faded into oblivion or ended up through age or aggression in the hell they richly deserve. Around the same time I was most closely following BTK, I also for the first time read writings by profilers that detailed the fact that some serials really do <i>almost</i> retire. Gary Ridgway never quite stopped, but he slowed way down. Now it&#39;s looking like Denny the Dog Catcher was similar - say Wegerle, Hedge, Davis really were his only &#34;other&#34; kills - that&#39;s not stopping, it&#39;s falling off the wagon. The model of addiction applies with serials, and perhaps it&#39;s possible that some of them as they age attempt to control their impulses, if for no other reason than to avoid jail. BTK and Zodiac were alike in their ability to control their impulses enough to know when to shut up - until Denny&#39;s purple diskette o&#39; death finally did do him in.  <BR> <BR>I don&#39;t know - I&#39;m just saying that it&#39;s a possibility worth exploring. Enough that it gave me the impetus to finally register with Tom&#39;s ZodiacKiller board. I actually did years ago but forgot the login! I&#39;ll be posting more about all this there when Tom gets around to approving my registration.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12550-->
<!--Post: 12551--><!--Time: 1112406901-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12551"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Ivy_leaguer<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Ivy_leaguer<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->10-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, April 01, 2005 - 5:55 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Tac, <BR> <BR>I am aware law enforcement will not renew their investigation without the discovery of new evidence or someone approaches them with promising information concerning the case. Any attempt to reignite the investigation would most probably be instigated by an amateur sleuth.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12551-->
<!--Post: 12555--><!--Time: 1112421329-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12555"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><a href="mailto:kathiemoffett@yahoo.com" target="_blank"><!--/email--><!--name-->Dahlia<!--/name--></b></a>
</a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Dahlia<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->3-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, April 01, 2005 - 9:55 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Steve, I&#39;m glad you&#39;re joining up with the Z gang. I&#39;ve been a lurking reader/socializer for a couple of years but only lately do I find myself wanting to post anything. Who knows why. I do think that BTK has attracted Tom and many of the other Zodiologists because he did exactly what we all can&#39;t help hoping Z will do someday. <BR> <BR>You interest me particularly because you don&#39;t seem to have one suspect you&#39;re totally involved with, but rather are choosing to keep an open mind at this point. I&#39;m much the same. I&#39;d love to believe it was Allen &#40;even though he&#39;s dead which would then be most frustrating!&#41; but there is indeed a fairly substantial body of information ruling him out. <BR> <BR>&#40;As an aside, &#39;cause I&#39;m rambling really and this came to mind...you might be particularly interested in Howard Davis&#39;s comprehensive research into the Kathleen Johns and Donna Lass stories. Much of it LE never even apparently got around to.&#41; <BR> <BR>I truly feel that Z is one that was crossed off a list or overlooked early on, or that perhaps he was never noticed at all. He strikes me as a &#39;still waters run deep&#39; sort, not an exhibitionist or overt eccentric like Allen. <BR> <BR>I too have a sense that Z is still somewhere around here or north of here &#40;I am in the Bay Area.&#41; The north coast of CA can be a strange place. A lot of folks have &#39;dropped out&#39; there.  <BR> <BR>And I also agree with you that there could be a copycat at work. Moreover, Cutler/Allen could be Z just as easily as Burgess or anyone else. Either way, I&#39;d like to find out more about Burgess--the draft-dodger point would seem to disqualify him. Certainly, even if Z weren&#39;t a Navy man, he would still IMHO have been a wanna-be &#40;like Rader was a wanna-be cop&#41;, or perhaps the son of a Navy man. Also, I think, from what little we&#39;ve heard, that Burgess is a little too conspicuously crazy to be Z. <BR> <BR>I have one problem with LE: if they want to close a case like Z, fine, but then &#40;holding back just enough to, as you say, weed out the false confessions AND insure a conviction if possible&#41; I think they should release the rest of what they&#39;ve got and let us go for it. Grrr. Well, that is if it&#39;s not all gone.  <BR> <BR>&#40;Do forgive me BTW, I figured you obviously knew all that about LE and so forth; I was being pedantic just &#39;cause I wasn&#39;t sure about everyone else.  Seems to me I read a lot of posts &#40;okay, maybe they were all on kansas.com!&#41; from folks who don&#39;t seem to get it.&#41; <BR> <BR>&#34;Purple diskette &#39;o death&#34;...snortle. Wouldn&#39;t you love to say that to his weasely little face?!<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12555-->
<!--Post: 12556--><!--Time: 1112421511-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12556"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><a href="mailto:bbyrum9@foothill.net" target="_blank"><!--/email--><!--name-->Nightdriver<!--/name--></b></a>
</a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Nightdriver<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->1-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, April 01, 2005 - 9:58 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Folks, the Stine case is not closed, just inactive. This was stated in a radio interview on KGO 810 on about April, 8, 2004 by SFPD brass. Tom was also interviewed for this piece. The reason given then was that the inspector who had that case was transferred out of Homicide, and that the case would be given to someone else when more guys were brought in the unit. <BR>The idea that &#34;new&#34; evidence can be found in re Z is kinda far out there. Rehashing the old evidence and seeing what might have been missed or mis-interpreted is much more likely and is always going on. Even that long piece about Kane is what, 11 years old? By the way, it was mentioned that Kane was involved with Allen Dorfman. Note that that piece doesn&#39;t say much of anything about mob involvement. Mr. Dorfman was really killed on Jan. 21, 1983 in the parking lot of the Lincolnwood Hyatt in Chicago.  <BR>Recently, two people posted on the A&amp;E discussion board about a person in a blue station wagon giving them a ride on US 101 in 1971 or 1972. The threads are interesting. Also, take a look at Doenetwork, case no. 454UMOH. Not an exact hit, but very interesting, nonetheless. <BR>My opinion of the similarities between Denny and Z is that Denny knows just as much about Z as I do, and I&#39;ve been looking at Z off and on for about 22 years. The posters here who have pointed out similarities in their writings are right on the money. The only difference between the two is that Denny&#39;s knowledge of codes and ciphers doesn&#39;t even come close to Z&#39;s.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12556-->
<!--Post: 12565--><!--Time: 1112426829-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12565"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Steve_huff<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Steve_huff<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, April 01, 2005 - 11:27 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text--><i>&#34;Denny knows just as much about Z as I do...&#34;</i> <BR> <BR>I agree - any one of us, &#40;im&#41;properly motivated enough, could ape Zodiac&#39;s style with a bit of study. I like puzzles and cryptograms myself a great deal, and even came up with a simple code that BTK could have used, had he had the brain he thought he did, that would have stumped tons of people and still been solved with the right info. <i>&#40;Basically, there is a known series of symbols that hobos used to use to indicate certain things to those who came behind them. A single symbol might represent a farm or house where the family was generous. These codes used as a substitute symbol for each letter of the alphabet might be extra confusing because anyone with a little knowledge about them could get very confused between the meaning of the actual symbol and whether it was being used to substitute for a letter or not. This idea o&#39; mine may or may not appear in a work of fiction one day. I ain&#39;t tellin&#39;.&#41;</i><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12565-->
<!--Post: 12576--><!--Time: 1112484787-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12576"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Tac<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Tac<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->11-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, April 02, 2005 - 3:33 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Ivy, <BR> <BR>Then where is the pressure you speak of that will make the Zodiac frightened enough to come out and communicate or start killing again ? <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12576-->
<!--Post: 12577--><!--Time: 1112485608-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12577"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Tac<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Tac<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->11-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, April 02, 2005 - 3:46 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Night, <BR> <BR>Excellent stuff !  I was the one that pointed out Allen Dorfman&#39;s capacity as the front man for the mob and their teamster&#39;s &#34;slush&#34; fund.  <BR> <BR>Dorfman, this is chronicled by the FBI, was a victim of a mob hit connected with legal problems and media exposure it brought. In addition his role in the mob &#34;skim&#34; was given as a reason for his murder.  .  .  <BR> <BR>Although my memory is fading on this here is the rest of why Dorfman was offed. A San Diego woman who invested significant money into a Dorfman/mob interest was getting cheated. She was the source of the aforementioned legal &#34;problems&#34; . This woman was murdered by the mob with Nicky Spilotro as the button man.  .  .  <!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12577-->
<!--Post: 12578--><!--Time: 1112485843-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12578"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Tac<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Tac<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->11-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, April 02, 2005 - 3:50 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Night, <BR> <BR>I find it very odd how SFPD would close the Zodiac case and leave the Stine case open.  .  .  <BR> <BR>I agree with you in that it would be crazy to think they are actually working on it or will solve the Stine murder.  .  . it&#39;s up to us amateurs. <BR> <BR>The Kane angle is very interesting. If he had mob ties might Kane have known the wild card: Ralph Spinelli ???<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12578-->
<!--Post: 12579--><!--Time: 1112487288-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12579"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Steve_huff<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Steve_huff<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, April 02, 2005 - 4:14 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->hey Night; <BR> <BR>I noticed &#34;Joe Chandler&#34; a while back. I have some weird thing for the doenetwork site - like I want to be haunted or something, so if I&#39;m up late and there&#39;s nothing else to read, I browse the doe network.  <BR> <BR>&#34;Joe Chandler&#34; is very intriguing indeed. One has to wonder if any of his personal effects, possibly complete with samples of his handwriting, were kept by authorities. <BR> <BR>I&#39;m glad you pointed it out, it was a good reminder for me personally. <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12579-->
<!--Post: 12581--><!--Time: 1112490336-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12581"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><a href="mailto:bbyrum9@foothill.net" target="_blank"><!--/email--><!--name-->Nightdriver<!--/name--></b></a>
</a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Nightdriver<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->1-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, April 02, 2005 - 5:05 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Steve: I&#39;ll bet SSA might have his original app. on file. How else would they know it was sent from Rapid City in 1978? Also his bank account application. Someone in LE here in Calif. who has a Z case is going to have to read that story and think maybe. That would make identifying him a criminal matter. <BR> <BR>Tac: Stine is the only case that is tied to Z right now in SFPD&#39;s jurisdiction. Therefore, if you had info about LHR, BRS, or LB, you would not present it to them, but rather to either Vallejo PD or Napa County Sheriff. That story about Kane was very intriguing, but nothing seems to have been followed up on. One thing is pretty sure. Kane did not do Kathleen Johns, or really LB. Descriptions given do not indicate anything abnormal about the perp&#39;s speech or walk. Kane was in a head-on with a truck in the South Bay area about 1957. He has walked with a limp and had a speech problem since then. Also KJ said that her abductor was about 26-30. Kane was born in 1924 to the best of my knowledge. It would be nice to see a documented connection between Kane and the mob.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12581-->
<!--Post: 12582--><!--Time: 1112491668-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12582"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Ivy_leaguer<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Ivy_leaguer<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->10-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, April 02, 2005 - 5:27 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Tac, <BR> <BR>The issue of exerting pressure on the unknown subject is complex and can be approached from different perspectives. One approach may involve an amateur sleuth discovering evidence suggesting the Zodiac is alive and informing a large media organization in California. It would have to maintain interest in the Zodiac case to have a small affect. It could begin with establishing a strong nexus between the murders in August 2004 and other known Zodiac murders and suggesting serial murderers do not necessarily cease murdering when they retreat from public attention.   <BR> <BR>Approaching the San Francisco Police Department and other police departments involved in the case would appear to be fruitless. They would only be interested in significant evidence resulting in the apprehension of the unknown subject. Internet web sites and message boards have limited appeal. The people who tend to visit Zodiac Killer web sites and write messages have an interest in the subject. They rarely attract the masses to create and sustain the pressure. Its not likely the Zodiac would respond to the pressure in the short term, but his behavior could change. He may become agitated, paranoid and fearful and it may become noticeable to those who know him or live near him.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12582-->
<!--Post: 12585--><!--Time: 1112495197-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12585"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Embersage<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Embersage<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, April 02, 2005 - 6:26 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I was advised yesterday to do research on Jack The Ripper to find patterns that Z followed. What would be your opinion on this matter. Thank You E.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12585-->
<!--Post: 12587--><!--Time: 1112500874-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12587"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Tac<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Tac<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->11-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, April 02, 2005 - 8:01 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Ivy, <BR> <BR>That is a huge stretch at best. Why would the Zodiac be intimidated by a amatuer when so many highly regarded detectives could not rattle his cage ?  <BR> <BR>Likewise why NOW would those around the Zodiac, this is all predicated on him being alive, suddenly notice a change in his behavior ?  Why would these people have not notice behavior changes during the killiing spree ?  <BR> <BR>You are not banking on the Zodiac to have grown a conscious ? A sociopath has many faces which he shows to different people in order to manipulate his way through life.  .  .   <BR> <BR>I seriously thought you had more to this idea than this.  .  .  <!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12587-->
<!--Post: 12593--><!--Time: 1112511283-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12593"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Elsbarn<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Elsbarn<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, April 02, 2005 - 10:54 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Dennis Rader would love to be given credit for being the Zodiac Killer. <BR> <BR>Both Rader and the Zodiac have different thought patterns from typical human beings, don&#39;t get me wrong, but the avenues that these thought patterns took point to two different killers. Both types result in social losers &#40;I really think the public&#39;s idea of Ted Bundy as a model for other serial killers did more to damage the  perception of propable perps in unknown serial crimes than Gary Ridgway did to correct it; meaning that the majority of serial killers are not good looking and suave&#41; and Son of Sam seems to me to be the personality type to align closest with Zodiac. Ridgway, in the end, aligned closest with BTK. It becomes a serial killer &#34;spectrum.&#34; <BR> <BR>We now know the presumption that serial killerc cannot stop is incorrect. Zodiac could very well be alive and well in California. <BR> <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12593-->
<!--Post: 12597--><!--Time: 1112514437-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12597"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Steve_huff<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Steve_huff<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, April 02, 2005 - 11:47 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text--><i>&#34;Dennis Rader would love to be given credit for being the Zodiac Killer.&#34;</i> <BR> <BR>Dennis Rader would love that more if the freedom from capture that Zodiac may still be enjoying <i>&#40;if he is not truly dead after all&#41;</i> came along with it. <BR> <BR>Denny was a slightly less complicated and probably less intelligent, or perhaps just less shrewd breed of psycho. Zodiac was eluding a much larger number of law enforcement officers, using jurisdictional boundaries against them and never sending that one smoking purple diskette o&#39; death like Denny finally slipped in the envelope that fateful day.  <BR> <BR>Honestly I think that says that where Denny had a criminal justice degree but never made it to being a cop, Zodiac may have had more of a taste of the actual job and a better-grounded knowledge of what police might do to pursue him. There&#39;s plenty of guys who while in the military were MPs who on the outside ended up doing something entirely unrelated to law enforcement - it&#39;s occurred to me before that Z was this type. Got his LE training with the navy &#40;that&#39;s my guess as to his affiliation, yes&#41; and on the outside went into something different, his MP training <i>&#40;or whatever they call the po-lice in da Navy&#41;</i> still lodged in his mind as his ace in the hole while dealing with civilian police.  <BR> <BR>I wrote off any relation other than admiration by Rader for the Zodiac a long time ago - people don&#39;t like to acknowledge that the main thing they had in common was taunting the cops in a spooky and cryptic manner. Other than that, Zodiac was a much more careful, shrewd, well-planned killer. Every site where the vics were a couple chosen well; deserted save the victims - at least as far as he could tell - his presence at the site except for Lake Berryessa was rather minimal - move in, shoot, shoot, shoot, move out.  <BR> <BR>Reading <i>Nightmare in Wichita</i> what does become clear is how close to inept Denny could get - in one letter, detailed in chapter Chapter 10, pg. 85, BTK wrote: <BR> <BR><i>&#34;...It take time to set a kill, one mistake and it all over. <b>Since I about blew it on the phone</b> - handwritting is out-letter guide is to long and type-writer can be traced too...&#34;</i> <BR> <BR>I left, as Beattie did, the errors in the original intact. Funny to see a serial actually say in a letter that he &#34;about blew it.&#34; That&#39;s another thing Z. probably would never have done; express that bizarre modesty. The ego structures, I&#39;m saying, while similar just by virtue of them both being serial killers, were different in some obvious and fundamental ways as well. Zodiac never wanted to compromise his sense of omnipotence. Even in the letter to Melvin Belli, Z is bemoaning his uncontrollable urges as if they are a terrible sort of power that overwhelms him.  <BR> <BR>BTK, however, pretty much said that he knew he came close to screwing up. It was, true, another way of rubbing WPD&#39;s face in it - the majority of that particular letter is virtually lecturing the cops on why they should have connected his crimes by that point. But admitting he could screw up was not a Zodiac thing to do. <!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12597-->
<!--Post: 12598--><!--Time: 1112516277-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12598"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Johno<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Johno<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Sunday, April 03, 2005 - 12:17 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I always have thought that the Zodiac symbol had a double meaning. <BR> Besides looking obviously like a gunsight it always reminded me of an intersection of two highways. Two highways cross and have a circlular drive around them with usually a clover leaf pattern in between but from above or a glance on a map it looks like a gunsight, especially with the roads extending beyond the circle like the Zodiac&#39;s symbol. The crosshairs not neatly within the circle like a true gunsight but extending beyond. <BR>I thought the Zodiac lived in Vallejo, California and his gunsight symbol was what is today where the 780 and 80 cross and he lived within a circle of that interchange near San Miguel road, Laurel street, Woodrow Avenue, Pine Street and Regis Avenue. He lived there and used freeways to get to and from his crime scenes. <BR>I thought BTK had some connection to the 135 through Wichita and he didn&#39;t necessarliy live near what was called the &#39;comfort zone&#39; of the killings but used the 135. It turned out he lived within sight of it. <BR>I have never posted over on the Tom&#39;s Zodiac board and am not really knowlegeable on all the specifics and suspects and theories of the Zodiac but this is what I have felt for a long time. When I learned where Dennis Rader lived in proportion to his accused crimes it made me think more about my Zodiac theory.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12598-->
<!--Post: 12600--><!--Time: 1112529843-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12600"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Ivy_leaguer<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Ivy_leaguer<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->10-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Sunday, April 03, 2005 - 5:04 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Tac, <BR> <BR>Strategic behavior provides the foundation for military strategies, criminal investigations and the decisions of corporations to merge with other companies, acquire them or compete with them. Strategic behavior is based on assumptions, otherwise strategies would not exist. This particular strategy assumes the Zodiac Killer is alive. Its one of four that I have formulated. The other three are in their infancy and will be revealed when they are complete. Consult any textbook on strategic behavior in economics, econometrics, psychology and psychometrics. The author will outline the assumptions used in the model to develop a strategy. <BR> <BR>The human psyche evolves during an individual&#39;s lifetime. During infancy, the baby is basically a body attempting to satisfy basic desires. Its preoccupied with consuming nutrition and exploring the world through the mouth. As a baby travels through childhood and adolescence the individual is taught to delay gratification. By adulthood, its assumed the individual has mastered the ability to control basic urges. When an individual enters later life the body deteriorates. The individual may lose control over bodily functions. Frailty and the decline in an individual&#39;s health has an affect on their psychology. The individual may no longer be confident to assume the risks of a younger adult. A person may have enjoyed playing tennis at 30, but is aware playing it at 65 could aggravate their arthritis and may demand too much running on the court. An elderly individual may require more time and thought to solve a problem in matrix algebra or solve the chain rule in calculus compared to when they were in college. Its possible they may no longer be able to solve the problem. <BR> <BR>The strategy postulated in my previous correspondence does not suggest the Zodiac Killer will develop a conscience. Psychopaths do not enjoy relinquishing control and domination of the situation. For the past 36 years the Zodiac Killer has retained control and domination of the murders he committed in northern California. When pressure is applied to a psychopath, they will attempt to regain control by exhibiting frustration, anger and agitation. They can develop paranoid thoughts and fear capture. Robert Hansen, the serial murderer who killed at least 17 prostitutes and exotic dancers in Alaska during the 1970s and early 1980s admitted to paranoid thoughts when the police investigation concentrated on him. He had nightmares that involved the police surrounding his home at night when he was asleep. He was afraid the police would arrest him and undermine his facade of respectability amongst the petty bourgeois of Anchorage, Alaska.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12600-->
<!--Post: 12608--><!--Time: 1112552771-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12608"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Steve_huff<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Steve_huff<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Sunday, April 03, 2005 - 11:26 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Ivy - thanks for supporting your thoughts with clear explanations and a good example. <BR> <BR>No, none of them develop a conscience. With age, the sexual drives that fuelled the crimes also diminish - just as those of us with a &#34;normal&#34; sexual psyche may notice a waxing and waning of &#34;normal&#34; desire, a sexually motivated serial may not feel such a powerful lust to kill anymore.  <BR> <BR>Another example to add here along Ivy&#39;s example of the Alaskan Baker/Serial Killer <i>&#40;Not the FBI Spy, by the way, for the terminally confused - that guy spelled his name Hans<b>s</b>en. Extra &#34;s&#34;&#41;</i> is Gary Ridgway. Authorities thought the Green River Killer ceased his activities in the late 80&#39;s. He didn&#39;t, but he did &#34;slow down&#34; and started consciously killing outside of his old zone when he did kill. He even says he tried to stop when he found &#34;religion,&#34; something many serials save for when they are trying to avoid the electric chair, it should be said. There are no atheists on death row, you know. <!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12608-->
<!--Post: 12615--><!--Time: 1112575636-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12615"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Ivy_leaguer<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Ivy_leaguer<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->10-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Sunday, April 03, 2005 - 5:47 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Steve, <BR> <BR>I agree with your analysis of sexual motivation and serial murder. Sexual arousal is intimately connected with serial murderers. Serial murderers tend to commit murder when they are sexually aroused. The level of sexual arousal can fluctuate. A serial murderer&#39;s sexual arousal can be high for several years and during this period the perpetrator tends to search for and murder victims to fulfil their violent sexual fantasies. The perpetrator&#39;s level of sexual arousal can decline and they may commit fewer murders or do not commit murder during the period. However, the level of sexual arousal may increase when they become older and the perpetrator may feel an urge to resume murdering. The perpetrator is likely to murder less victims when they become older. A decline in sexual arousal tends to be the predominant factor. Dennis Rader is accused of murdering Marine Hedge in April 1985 when he was 40 years old and murdering Dolores Davis in 1991 when he was almost 46 years old. He admitted to murdering Vicki Wegerle when he was 41. At the moment Rader is accused of seven murders between 1974 and 1977 and three murders between 1985 and 1991. Human psychology is a complex discourse and deriving generalizations from research can be difficult. <!--/Text--></td></tr>
