<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12622"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Embersage<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Embersage<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Monday, April 04, 2005 - 1:31 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Steve,  I was told when I started on the Z board to read info from the past Five years, so I would not bring up stuff previously covered. You know like how we get it here sometimes. Then I read your post today, good work and ask Ed. Thanks Sheila<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12622-->
<!--Post: 12630--><!--Time: 1112629362-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12630"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Steve_huff<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Steve_huff<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Monday, April 04, 2005 - 8:42 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->First, may I say that to post of these matters with the funeral music for His Holiness Pope John Paul II playing behind me is to know the definition of eerie. <BR> <BR>That said...<a href="http://www.catchbtk.com/discus/messages/1/28.html#POST12556">Nightdriver brought up earlier</a> the case of <a href="mailto:starlet2367@bellsouth.net">doenetwork.org&#39;s 454umoh</a>. <BR> <BR>The man in question died by his own hand in 2002, and it was discovered that the name he was using, Joseph Newton Chandler, was false. The real Joseph Chandler was killed in an auto accident with his parents in 1945.  <BR> <BR>The man is on the doenetwork&#39;s page because tracing his truth has proven difficult. The best authorities have been able to do is the following; <BR> <BR>- he had an electrical engineering background <BR>- he was former navy <BR>- he left California prior to assuming this name, around <b>1978</b>. <BR> <BR>He was very good at covering up who he really was. <BR> <BR>Nightdriver noted, and I agree, some reason to wonder about this guy with Zodiac in mind - the navy association, the use of a high-caliber firearm to off himself, in this case, the engineering background, coming from California... <BR> <BR>Now I&#39;ve found an even better reason to wonder.  <BR> <BR>We all know that Zodiac and thence BTK took pages of their playbooks from the OSK, or &#34;original&#34; ess kay, Jack the Ripper <i>&#40;before any wag reminds me that saucy Jacky was not truly the first SK, shut up, I know&#41;</i>. Contacting the police, taunting the police, sending evidence - the Ripper sent body parts, among other things - striking in a manner that allowed him to permanently avoid capture...ya get the picture.  <BR> <BR>Well, peeps, I kept thinking that the name Joseph Chandler rang a freakin&#39; bell. I now know why. <BR> <BR>Everyone who knows their serial killer history will know that Inspector Abberline is the name of the London detective historically most closely associated with the Ripper investigation. <BR> <BR>What many may have forgotten is that another Inspector, the Metropolitan Police&#39;s H Division lead on the case, was named... <BR> <BR><b><i>Joseph Chandler.</i></b> <BR> <BR>Ripperology is an old art among true crime afficionados. This information has been available for as long as the Ripper investigation has been picked apart by amateur sleuths such as we. <BR> <BR>From the website, <a href="http://www.casebook.org/witnesses/bayleys.html">Casebook: Jack the Ripper</a>; <BR> <BR><i>&#34;...Inspector Joseph Chandler was asked about these discoveries on the morning of 12 September: <BR> <BR>    A Star reporter called on Inspector Chandler this morning to know whether this startling story had any basis in fact. The inspector hadn&#39;t seen the statement, and when it was read to him he nearly laughed. And no wonder; the supposed bloodstains are only the discolorations on the mortar caused by urine! As for blood on the bricks, and the bloodstained paper, nothing of the kind had been found. &#40;Star - 12 September 1888&#41;...&#34;</i> <BR> <BR>Anyone else think I&#39;m onto something? <BR> <BR>I&#39;m e-mailing Ed, ember, thanks for the reminder. I now need to hie myself hither to ZodiacKiller.com quick - though I think this post is important to make, it&#39;s close to getting off-topic, and I respect Tom&#39;s feelings about that sort of thing a great deal, have always admired the way he policed it. <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12630-->
<!--Post: 12631--><!--Time: 1112640671-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12631"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><a href="mailto:bbyrum9@foothill.net" target="_blank"><!--/email--><!--name-->Nightdriver<!--/name--></b></a>
</a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Nightdriver<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->1-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Monday, April 04, 2005 - 11:51 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Mr. Huff, you are outrageous. If you aren&#39;t associated with LE in some way, you should be. No, maybe not not, you might outshine too many of them. One could say that, no, he picked that name because it was a young male about his age, AND that both parents were also killed. That would lessen the chance that another member of their family might see the name. But, the app was sent from Rapid City in 1978 and the accident occured on US 82 where? This person was supposed to have only come to the Cleveland area in 1979. Also young Joseph Chandler most certainly wasn&#39;t the only young man about that age who died, long before this person needed a new identity. Most likely many of the others by 1978 would also have no other living relatives. The choice of Joe ChandlerIII is probably not coincidental.  <BR>The questions about serials following one another in some way is probably correct. I feel that the factors that make these people who they are are common, but that each does adapt in some way to the local circumstances. That makes it hard to take the MO of one several years previous and carry it forward in an attempt to identify a current one. I was watching that show about the Black Daliah and when I saw that letter written to the Times I said &#34;holy bleep, is that where Z got his style of felt tip lettering from?&#34; <BR>I think the important thing right now is the period 1971-1978. We don&#39;t see from the recap anything before Rapid City, and one could assume that he most likely did not use that name before the original SSA application. Until we can be pretty sure that Z &#34;stopped&#34; when we think, I&#39;d say that anything between Calif. and Rapid City and Cleveland during the requisite period is still on the table. Before you start laughing, remember one thing: NO criminal investigation in re Joe Chandler seems to have ever been instigated.  <BR>To put a little Denny in this, Steve. Now that we may be able to say that the self-proclaimed &#34;bio&#34; is not relevant to him, have you thought about trying to determine if it may be on target for someone else from the Wichita area? Perhaps someone Denny did know, and may now be deceased? I find it kinda woo-woo that he would give a bio of a living person who could possibly be found and may know that &#34;Hey, Denny knew all that about me.&#34;  All in all, fantastic work Steve. Can you just imagine you and Skov working together? We would be inundated with intel day and night.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
